hat  undergraduates do with it and so we  wanted to look at these trade-offs in  terms of will will the law enforcement  subjects go after the drugs or will they  wait and try to get the cash in order to  fight more crime or will they just take  the cash and that's it in bart Wilson's  world for undergraduate students move  avatars around a 2d game board the red  avatar represents law enforcement the  sharing the three blue avatars represent  the public and they mostly scoot around  picking up tokens were three cents each  that is they accumulate wealth the  sheriff gets paid for collecting tokens  and for knocking down walls with hammers  that knocking down walls is like  fighting crime inside the walls are more  tokens some are red tokens that only the  sheriff can pick up these are assets  like drug money off limits to the  general public pastel tokens represent  the public benefits of a safer crime  free neighborhood only blue avatars can  acquire those the brightly colored  tokens represent another kind of  property the cops concedes like the  house of a woman whose son sells drugs  on the porch without her knowledge or  the cash seized during a routine highway  stop property that the police could  relinquish to the public or keep for  themselves as soon as you give them  civil forfeiture they are collecting 95  to ninety seven percent of all those  tokens they barely have any there for  the public maybe it seems obvious that  most people will keep the money rather  than handing it over to others but  police department's argue just the  opposite when they say that civil  forfeiture is still a win-win because  police use the seized property to invest  in more crime fight so this version of  the game tests those incentives the  sheriff can use the money earned from  solving a crime and seizing illegal  assets to buy another hammer break  another wall and give the public access  to their tokens but doing that would eat  into his profits by as much as six cents  ho